@database "context_engineer"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: context_engineer.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Context Engineer"
@{h1}Context Engineer

A long agent run fills the model's context with tool results, files,
images and old turns until it gets expensive or no longer fits. The
Context Engineer keeps it in bounds without losing what it takes out:
before an LLM call it moves large tool results, attached files and media
into stores on disk and leaves a short placeholder in their place; when
that is not enough it archives or removes old messages -- written to the
archive first. The agent gets tools to look through what was moved out
and read it back piece by piece, and a small core memory for facts it
must not lose. The @{b}Context Engineer@{ub} panel shows every
compaction, what a session's stores hold and its facts.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Context Engineer panel with the compactions and facts of a session"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Context Engineer panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The list tool  " link list}
  @{"  The read tool  " link read}
  @{"  The store_fact tool  " link store_fact}
  @{"  The compact tool and /compact  " link compact}
  @{"  The compaction hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What a compaction does  " link layers}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Context Engineer panel"
@{h1}The Context Engineer panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Context & memory@{ub}, "Context
Engineer"), or from a session's info button, which opens it on that
session.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Context Engineer panel"}

@{h2}Which data it shows

The panel follows the chat: it shows the session open there and switches
with it. Opened from a session's info button it stays on that session --
the toolbar says so, and @{b}Follow the chat@{ub} lets go of it. @{b}All
sessions@{ub} shows the compactions of every session, with a column
naming the session, and no stores; that view is an admin's, anyone else
gets "The context engineering could not be loaded" and the reason. With
no session open the panel says so and asks for nothing. Another user's
session looks like one where nothing happened.

The panel reloads every 10 seconds; the menu next to the reload button
sets another interval or turns it off.

@{h2}What you see

@{bullet}Four figures over every compaction of the session (or of all):
how many, the tokens saved, the average reduction, and the media items
taken out -- split into the media window (always_compact_media_keep_last),
duplicates, and other events.
@{bullet}For a session, three more from its stores on disk: the stored
tool results and attached files, the archived messages, and the core
memory facts, each with its tokens -- the facts against the core memory
budget.
@{bullet}@{b}Core memory@{ub}: the session's facts with category and
importance, by category, the most important first.
@{bullet}@{b}Compactions@{ub}: the newest 100, newest first -- a note
above the table says when there are more. Each row has the time, the
agent, the tokens before and after, the tokens saved, the reduction, the
tool results stored, the media taken out (with the megabytes) and the
layers that ran. A click on a column head sorts by it.

The layer badges (hover one for its meaning):

@{table}
Badge | Means
T | a new tool result too large for the window was stored on arrival
P | over the message limit, the oldest messages were archived and removed
M | only the newest messages with media keep it
B | the request was over its size limit; large media was taken out
L1 | tool results, attached files and inline media stored away
L2 | old messages archived and replaced by a placeholder with a summary
L3 | old messages archived and removed
@{body}

Only a run that changed something is listed. The list is kept in
@{tt}data/context_engineer/history.json@{utt}, the last 1000, and
survives a restart. It is the server process's list: a compaction in an
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} run is written to the same file by that process, but
the panel does not show it, and the next save of one process overwrites
the other's.

The panel only reads; nothing you do there changes a session. A store
that cannot be read answers with the reason, and the panel shows it.
@endnode

@node list "The list tool"
@{h1}The list tool

@{tt}context_engineer_list@{utt} (named after the plugin instance) shows
what was moved out of the agent's own conversation: one line per item
with its ref, size and a summary, never the whole text. With
@{tt}filter@{utt} it searches instead and adds the matching excerpt to
each line -- often the whole answer.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
section | history (archived messages), tool_results (stored tool results and attached files), facts (core memory), all; without it history when browsing, all with a filter; all needs a filter
filter | words to look for; without it the tool pages through one section
offset | browsing only: none for the newest page, negative counts back from the end, 0 or more is a position from the start
limit | 1 to 50 lines, per section when filtering; without it 20
role | history only: user, assistant or tool
@{body}

Every section is in order of storing, oldest first. How a filter
matches in history: with semantic search on (the shipped configuration),
the messages closest in meaning together with those holding every word;
when the meaning search finds nothing, or semantic search is off, the
messages holding any of the words, ranked (and, when no whole word
matches, the filter as a piece of text). In tool_results and facts the
filter matches as one piece of text, case ignored. With a role, the
history search keeps only that role's messages among the ones it found.

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}@{tt}entries@{utt}, each with @{tt}ref@{utt} and
@{tt}kind@{utt}: a message with its role, tool, @{tt}tokens@{utt},
@{tt}at@{utt} and @{tt}summary@{utt}; a tool result with its tool,
@{tt}tokens@{utt}, @{tt}at@{utt}, @{tt}chars@{utt} and a
@{tt}summary@{utt} -- with a filter @{tt}match@{utt} in its place; a
fact with its category, importance and @{tt}summary@{utt} (the fact
itself), no ref, tokens or time. With a filter messages and facts carry
@{tt}match@{utt} as well.
@{bullet}Browsing adds @{tt}total@{utt}, @{tt}offset@{utt},
@{tt}has_more_before@{utt}, @{tt}has_more_after@{utt} and
@{tt}next_offset@{utt}; a filter adds a @{tt}hint@{utt} instead.
@{bullet}Refused: an unknown section (with the valid ones) and
@{tt}section='all'@{utt} without a filter, as @{tt}{"status": "error",
"error": ...}@{utt}.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{}
{"offset": -50}
{"section": "tool_results"}
{"filter": "QS-4711"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node read "The read tool"
@{h1}The read tool

@{tt}context_engineer_read@{utt} reads one stored item by its ref -- a
piece at a time, so reading it does not put the whole thing back into the
context it was taken out of.

@{h2}Refs

@{table}
Ref | Is
arch_... | an archived message
TR_... | a stored tool result or attached file; its tool_call id (call_... or toolu_...) or the 8-character content_hash of the placeholder work too
a file path | a media file to load back into the conversation
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
ref | the ref, from list or from a placeholder in the conversation
offset | where to start, in characters; negative counts back from the end (-2000 = the last 2000); 0 without it
limit | characters to return, up to 5000; 2000 without it (0 as well)
find | instead of a piece: the places that contain this text, up to 12, each with 200 characters around it
@{body}

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}A piece: @{tt}content@{utt}, @{tt}offset@{utt},
@{tt}returned_chars@{utt}, @{tt}total_chars@{utt},
@{tt}truncated@{utt}, @{tt}has_more_before@{utt},
@{tt}has_more_after@{utt} and @{tt}next_offset@{utt} to continue, with
the item's tool or role, tokens, time and summary.
@{bullet}With find: @{tt}matches@{utt} (@{tt}at@{utt} and
@{tt}snippet@{utt}), @{tt}match_count@{utt} and
@{tt}more_matches@{utt}; offset and limit do not apply.
@{bullet}A media path: "File will be loaded in the next response.
Estimated cost: ~N tokens" -- the file is shown to the model on its next
call. Only files under the data directory are loaded; any other path is
refused ("Path is outside the allowed media directories."), as is a file
that is gone or of a type it does not know.
@{bullet}An archived message that points to another archived message is
followed to the text; the answer then says @{tt}resolved_ref@{utt}.
@{bullet}Refused: no ref, a ref of no known shape, a ref not found --
each as @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ..., "hint": ...}@{utt} naming
where valid refs come from.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"ref": "TR_4f1c2a9b3e", "find": "Traceback"}
{"ref": "TR_4f1c2a9b3e", "offset": -2000}
{"ref": "arch_9a0c3b17e2d4"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node store_fact "The store_fact tool"
@{h1}The store_fact tool

@{tt}context_engineer_store_fact@{utt} keeps a fact in the session's core
memory: a decision, a preference, something the agent must not lose when
the messages around it are compacted. The facts are shown to the model in
the note the hook adds (see @{"  What the model sees  " link model}) and
listed by @{tt}list(section='facts')@{utt}.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
fact | the text; required, and not blank
category | decisions, preferences, facts, context or tasks; without it, or with any other value, facts
importance | 0.0 to 1.0, clamped; without it 0.5
@{body}

@{h2}The budget

A session's facts share @{tt}core_memory_max_tokens@{utt} (2000 by
default, 5000 in the shipped configuration). When a new fact does not fit,
it pushes out facts that are not more important than it, least important
and then oldest first; if those do not make room, it is refused and
nothing is pushed out. The same text stored again (case ignored) is not
added twice; a higher importance raises the kept one.

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}@{tt}status: "success"@{utt} with the @{tt}category@{utt} and
@{tt}importance@{utt} the fact is kept under and @{tt}total_facts@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}status: "error"@{utt}: "Fact parameter is required" (also for a blank fact),
"This fact alone is larger than core memory (N tokens); store a shorter
summary of it.", or "Core memory is full of more important facts; this
one was not stored. Use a higher importance if it matters more."

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"fact": "The API runs on port 8000 behind nginx", "category": "facts", "importance": 0.8}
@{body}
@endnode

@node compact "The compact tool and /compact"
@{h1}The compact tool and /compact

@{tt}context_engineer_compact@{utt} (no parameters) runs the compaction
hook now on the running conversation, with the agent's own settings.
Typed at the chat prompt as @{tt}/compact@{utt} it is available where
the agent's allowlist has the tool.

@{bullet}@{b}Called by the model@{ub} the automatic rules apply: below
the thresholds, or while the hysteresis holds, no layer runs. The passes
that run on every call still do -- with @{tt}always_compact_media_keep_last@{utt}
above 0 (the shipped configuration) the media window and the duplicate
media, and over @{tt}max_messages@{utt} Pre-Layer P unless held.
@{bullet}@{b}/compact typed by a person@{ub} skips the thresholds and the
hysteresis: Layer 1 runs whatever the token count, the message limit and
the media passes apply; Layers 2 and 3 only above their thresholds (see
@{"  What a compaction does  " link layers}).

@{h2}Answers

@{tt}status: "success"@{utt} with @{tt}modified@{utt},
@{tt}original_tokens@{utt}, @{tt}final_tokens@{utt},
@{tt}tokens_saved@{utt}, @{tt}reduction_percent@{utt},
@{tt}layers_applied@{utt}, @{tt}tool_results_stored@{utt},
@{tt}messages_archived@{utt}, @{tt}messages_dropped@{utt} and
@{tt}messages_pruned@{utt}. A run turned away adds @{tt}reason@{utt}
(@{tt}below_threshold@{utt} or @{tt}hysteresis@{utt}), with the current
reading as both token counts. An empty conversation answers "No messages
in conversation"; without a session @{tt}{"status": "error", "error":
"Session context not available"}@{utt}.

It works also for an agent whose hook is switched off: then the tool is
the only way a compaction happens.
@endnode

@node hook "The compaction hook"
@{h1}The compaction hook

@{tt}engineer_context@{utt} runs before each LLM call of an agent that
switches it on -- it is off by default, on for @{tt}multi_turn_agent@{utt}
and the agents built on it. It runs before context_summarizer's hook.

@{h2}Switching it on

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      context_engineer.engineer_context:
        enabled: true
        tool_result_keep_last: 8      # any compaction setting, for this agent
@{body}

Every compaction setting of @{"  Setting it up  " link setup} can be set
here for one agent; a key left blank keeps the plugin's value. Six are
the plugin's alone and are ignored here (logged): session_ttl_seconds,
max_tracked_sessions, session_data_ttl_days, enable_semantic_search,
core_memory_max_tokens, storage_path. A misspelled key is logged too.

@{h2}The reading

The hook measures the call as the larger of the prompt tokens of the
session's last LLM call (from context_usage_tracker, unless a compaction
has made that stale) and an estimate of the messages plus the tool
definitions. The window is the answering model's.

@{h2}When it does something

A call is taken up when any of these holds; otherwise the hook changes
nothing, and adds nothing:

@{bullet}the reading is at @{tt}layer1_threshold@{utt} or above;
@{bullet}the request is larger than @{tt}max_request_bytes@{utt};
@{bullet}the conversation has more messages than @{tt}max_messages@{utt}
(when that is above 0);
@{bullet}@{tt}always_compact_media_keep_last@{utt} is above 0 (the
shipped configuration: 2, so every call is taken up);
@{bullet}the last message is from a person and
@{tt}compact_media_after_user_message@{utt} is on;
@{bullet}a new tool result is due for Pre-Layer T (see
@{"  What a compaction does  " link layers}) -- then only T runs.

@{h2}The hysteresis

Every rewrite of old messages breaks the provider's prompt cache from
there on, so after a run of Pre-Layer P or a layer the next such run waits
until the reading has grown by @{tt}min_tokens_between_compactions@{utt}
(20000) since -- unless a deeper layer has become due, the byte limit is
exceeded, or a person typed /compact. The media passes and Pre-Layer T are
not held.

@{h2}What the chat shows

A run that changed something shows a status line: "Engineering context:
152000 tokens (target: 70000)", then "Context engineered: saved 81200
tokens (53.4%), 12 tool result(s) stored, 2 media item(s) compacted". A
run that changed nothing shows none.
@endnode

@node layers "What a compaction does"
@{h1}What a compaction does

The passes run in this order. What leaves the conversation is written to
a store first; if that write fails, the messages stay.

@{h2}Pre-Layer T: oversized new results

A tool result of the current round (after the model's last answer) larger
than @{tt}tool_result_max_window_share@{utt} (0.25) of the window is
stored before any request carries it, whatever the thresholds and the
hysteresis say. Only that round is touched, so what earlier requests sent
stays as it was.

With @{tt}tool_result_summary_from@{utt} above 0, a new result with at
least that many tokens of prose is stored the same way, and a cheap model
(@{tt}tool_result_summary_profile@{utt}, "summarizer") writes a summary
into the placeholder -- for an agent handed long answers by sub-agents.
@{tt}tool_result_summary_tools@{utt} limits it to tools by name pattern
(@{tt}*_manage_sub_agent@{utt}); empty means every tool. A structured
(JSON) result is not summarized; a wrapper around prose is summarized by
its prose. The model reads the first 60000 characters; a summary longer
than half the prose, or all summaries of one round past 20 seconds, fall
back to the plain placeholder. The result goes to that profile's
provider.

@{h2}Pre-Layer P: the message limit

Over @{tt}max_messages@{utt} messages (0: off), the oldest are archived
and removed down to @{tt}max_messages_prune_to@{utt} (0, or a value above
max_messages: half the limit). Among the oldest, placeholders go first.
A single system note, @{tt}pruned_notice@{utt}, says how many have left
in all and how to find them.

@{h2}The media passes

@{bullet}@{b}M@{ub} (@{tt}always_compact_media_keep_last@{utt} N, 0 off):
when more than N messages carry images, audio or video, only the newest
N minus @{tt}always_compact_media_headroom@{utt} (at least 1) keep theirs.
@{bullet}@{b}Duplicates@{ub} (@{tt}deduplicate_media@{utt}, on): the same
file or the same bytes twice -- every copy but the newest goes.
@{bullet}@{b}After a new message from a person@{ub}
(@{tt}compact_media_after_user_message@{utt}, only while M is off): all
media outside the newest message and the current request.
@{bullet}@{b}B@{ub}: still over @{tt}max_request_bytes@{utt}, every item
of 10 KB or more outside the last two messages.

An inline item is saved to disk first (with
@{tt}store_media_before_compaction@{utt}, kept
@{tt}media_store_ttl_seconds@{utt} since its last use, at most
@{tt}media_store_max_files@{utt} per session); in its place stands a hint
naming the file, which read loads again. An image given only by a remote
URL stays. @{tt}compact_media_after_final_response@{utt} has no effect.

@{h2}Layer 1: reversible

From @{tt}layer1_threshold@{utt} on (or /compact, or the byte limit):

@{bullet}A tool result of at least @{tt}tool_result_min_size@{utt} tokens
is stored when it is older than the newest
@{tt}tool_result_keep_last@{utt} results, or larger than
@{tt}tool_result_max_inline_size@{utt} even if it is among them.
@{bullet}Files attached to a message (text files) of that size go to the
same store; so does inline media of that size, except in the current
request's messages.
@{bullet}Media files attached to messages, of that size, are taken out,
except on the newest @{tt}tool_result_keep_last@{utt} tool results --
over the byte limit on those too.

The answers of list and read are never stored away: the agent just
fetched them. When Layer 1 brings the reading to @{tt}target_tokens@{utt}
and the request to @{tt}target_request_bytes@{utt}, the run ends here.

@{h2}Layer 2: archive old turns

From @{tt}layer2_threshold@{utt}: messages at least
@{tt}archive_after_turns@{utt} turns old (a turn begins with a message
from a person) are archived and replaced in place by an
@{tt}archived_ref@{utt} placeholder with a short summary (at most
@{tt}max_summary_tokens@{utt}). A tool call and its results go together;
system messages stay while @{tt}keep_system_messages@{utt} is on. The
shipped configuration sets 9999, which switches this layer off in
practice.

@{h2}Layer 3: remove

From @{tt}layer3_threshold@{utt}: messages at least
@{tt}drop_after_turns@{utt} turns old are archived and removed, and then
more of the oldest until the estimate is at @{tt}target_tokens@{utt}.
Always kept: system messages (with keep_system_messages), the prune note,
the first input, the current request's messages, the round the model has
not seen yet, and the newest @{tt}tool_result_keep_last@{utt} units
before it.

@{h2}After a run

After a removal the conversation must still open with an input; messages
left in front of the first one are archived and removed too. Provider
reasoning (encrypted reasoning, thought signatures)
is marked unusable from the first changed message on; the session's
measured token figures are marked stale.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The tool descriptions and parameter descriptions come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; the answers and error texts are in the tool
nodes. The call's status line ends with its result: "history: 20 of 143
(from #123, 123 older)", "'QS-4711': 2 message, 1 tool_result",
"TR_4f1c2a9b3e: 2000 of 9340 chars, 7340 left", "Stored fact: The API
runs on port 8000 behind nginx...", "Compacted: saved 81200 tokens (53.4%
reduction)".

@{h2}Placeholders

Where a tool result was stored:

@{code json}
{"type": "tool_result_ref", "tool_name": "read_file", "ref_id": "TR_4f1c2a9b3e", "content_hash": "9f86d081", "token_count": 12480}
@{body}

-- with a @{tt}summary@{utt} when Pre-Layer T had one written. Where a
message was archived, in its own role:
@{tt}{"type": "archived_ref", "ref_id": "arch_...", "summary": "User:
..."}@{utt}. Where media was: @{tt}[Image 'plot.png' compacted.
read(ref="...") loads it again.]@{utt}. Where messages were pruned or
removed, one system message at the front with
@{tt}"type": "pruned_notice"@{utt}, the total and how to find them.

@{h2}The stored-information note

On a call the hook takes up, and when the session has something stored or
a fact, a note from the plugin is added at the end of what goes to the
model -- not saved with the conversation, and not added again while an
identical one is there:

@{code markdown}
# Context Engineer - Stored Information

Some information has been stored externally to save context space. Use the provided tools to retrieve full content when needed.

## Tool Results
Some tool results have been stored externally. When you see a JSON reference with `type: tool_result_ref`, use list(section='tool_results') ...

## Conversation Archive
Older messages have been archived. ...

<core_memory>
## Decisions
- Never deploy on Fridays
</core_memory>
@{body}

The session keeps the note it was first shown, so the note does not
change on every call: it is written anew only when messages were
archived or removed, when something else (context_summarizer) rewrote the
conversation, or when the first tool results were stored. A fact stored in
between is in the conversation as the store_fact call until then.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Everything the hook does before the model's last answer rewrites old
messages and breaks the provider's prompt cache from the first changed
message on; the next call pays the rest of the conversation once. That is
why the layers cut deep (to @{tt}target_tokens@{utt}, to
@{tt}max_messages_prune_to@{utt}) and the hysteresis spaces them out.
Pre-Layer T touches only the round no request has carried yet, and the
note stands at the end: neither breaks the cache.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}The panel's compaction list belongs to the server process: a
compaction in an @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} run is not shown, and the two
processes overwrite each other's saves of @{tt}history.json@{utt}.
@{bullet}The media store keeps a file @{tt}media_store_ttl_seconds@{utt}
after its last use; a hint older than that names a file that is gone.
@{bullet}The stores of a session keep the settings they were created
with; a media store setting changed for an agent reaches only sessions it
starts.
@{bullet}Without a known context window the size cap of Pre-Layer T
does not run; its summaries (tool_result_summary_from) still do.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    context_engineer:
      type: context_engineer
      enabled: true
      config:
        layer1_threshold: 140000
        layer2_threshold: 170000
        layer3_threshold: 200000
        target_tokens: 70000
        min_tokens_between_compactions: 20000
        store_media_before_compaction: true
        media_store_ttl_seconds: 604800
        media_store_max_files: 500
        max_request_bytes: 30408704
        target_request_bytes: 25165824
        core_memory_max_tokens: 5000
        tool_result_min_size: 500
        tool_result_keep_last: 5
        tool_result_max_inline_size: 10000
        archive_after_turns: 9999
        keep_system_messages: true
        enable_semantic_search: true
        session_data_ttl_days: 14
        drop_after_turns: 30
        max_summary_tokens: 100
        max_messages: 190
        max_messages_prune_to: 100
        deduplicate_media: true
        compact_media_after_user_message: true
        compact_media_after_final_response: true
        always_compact_media_keep_last: 2
        always_compact_media_headroom: 1
@{body}

The hook runs for an agent that switches it on (see
@{"  The compaction hook  " link hook}); the tools only for an agent
whose allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+context_engineer/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Server settings

These go under @{tt}config:@{utt} in the server entry; what is not set
there takes the default below. All but the last six can be set per agent.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
layer1_threshold | 80000 | tokens from which Layer 1 runs
layer2_threshold | 100000 | tokens from which Layer 2 runs
layer3_threshold | 120000 | tokens from which Layer 3 runs
target_tokens | 60000 | where the layers stop
max_request_bytes | 94371840 (90 MB) | a larger request is compacted whatever the tokens
target_request_bytes | 73400320 (70 MB) | Layer 1 ends only below this too
tool_result_min_size | 500 | tokens a tool result, file or media item must have to be stored
tool_result_keep_last | 3 | newest tool results Layer 1 keeps (unless too large)
tool_result_max_inline_size | 5000 | tokens from which Layer 1 stores a result even among the newest
tool_result_max_window_share | 0.25 | Pre-Layer T: share of the window a new result may take; 0 off
tool_result_summary_from | 0 | Pre-Layer T: prose tokens from which a new result is summarized; 0 off
tool_result_summary_profile | summarizer | the llm profile that writes those summaries; empty: none
tool_result_summary_tools | [] | tool name patterns that may be summarized; empty: every tool
archive_after_turns | 10 | Layer 2: age in turns
keep_system_messages | true | system messages are never archived or removed
drop_after_turns | 50 | Layer 3: age in turns
max_summary_tokens | 100 | length of an archive placeholder's summary
max_messages | 0 | Pre-Layer P: message limit; 0 off
max_messages_prune_to | 0 | Pre-Layer P: messages kept; 0: half the limit
min_tokens_between_compactions | 20000 | the hysteresis
deduplicate_media | true | remove older copies of the same media
compact_media_after_user_message | false | take media out when a person writes
compact_media_after_final_response | false | no effect
always_compact_media_keep_last | 0 | messages with media that keep it; 0 off
always_compact_media_headroom | 0 | how far below keep_last an eviction goes
store_media_before_compaction | true | save inline media to disk before taking it out
media_store_ttl_seconds | 604800 | how long a saved media file is kept after its last use
media_store_max_files | 500 | saved media files per session, the oldest go first
session_ttl_seconds | 7200 | a session's open stores are closed after this idle time
max_tracked_sessions | 100 | sessions kept open, the least recently used closed first
session_data_ttl_days | 0 | delete a session's stored data after this many idle days; 0 off
enable_semantic_search | false | search the archive by meaning as well (a vector store)
core_memory_max_tokens | 2000 | the core memory budget per session
storage_path | data/context_engineer | where the stores lie
@{body}

A misspelled key is logged at start and reaches nothing.

@{h2}Where data lives

Each session has a folder named after it under the storage path:
@{tt}tool_results.db@{utt} (tool results and attached files),
@{tt}archive.db@{utt} (archived messages), @{tt}core_memory.json@{utt} and
@{tt}media/@{utt} (saved media). With semantic search on and chromadb
installed, the vectors of all sessions lie in @{tt}_shared_vectors/@{utt};
with another vector backend each session keeps its own in
@{tt}vectors/@{utt}. With @{tt}session_data_ttl_days@{utt} set (the
shipped configuration: 14), a sweep at most once an hour deletes the
folders -- and vectors -- of sessions idle longer than that. The panel's
compaction list is @{tt}data/context_engineer/history.json@{utt}.

@{h2}Behind the panel

@{table}
Method | Path | Answer
GET | /plugins/context_engineer/history?session_id=&limit= | the newest limit compactions (1 to 1000, default 100) and the figures over all of them
GET | /plugins/context_engineer/session?session_id= | what the session's stores hold: tool_results and archived (count, tokens), core_memory (facts, tokens, max_tokens); 503 when a store cannot be read
@{body}

A user sees her own sessions; another user's session answers as one with
nothing in it. All sessions at once is an admin's (403 otherwise).
@endnode
